WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:01.990
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello,
my name's Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:03.323
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:03.323 --> 00:00:05.140
And I'm here with
Michelle Casbon.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.720
And she is in
developer relations,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.940
working on the Kubeflow project.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.090
We're currently on the
path to Kubeflow 1.0.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.050
It's coming soon.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.710
What can we expect to
see in Kubeflow 1.0?

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.590
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, so we
just rolled our 0.5 release

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.650
a few months ago.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.310
And we're preparing
for 0.6, which is sort

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.680
of the last leg towards 1.0.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.610
And we're really focusing on
taking a lot of the learnings

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.400
from our initial
users, so the ones that

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.150
have been with us from
the very beginning.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.760
Kubeflow is about a year and
a half old at this point.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.820
We launched in KubeCon
Austin, a year and a half ago.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.200
And at this point, we're
focusing on hardening our APIs.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.390
So we've been
listening to our users.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.770
We have a great
contributor base,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.312
and they've given us a lot of
feedback on what works for them

00:00:50.312 --> 00:00:52.207
and what doesn't, which
features they like,

00:00:52.207 --> 00:00:53.290
which features they don't.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.180
And so now we're
hardening those,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.310
we're making sure that
people who've installed it

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.640
can upgrade in a
seamless way, enterprise

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.750
features like multi-tenancy,
more individualized,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.370
granular security controls,
that kind of thing.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.990
So making it a robust
experience for enterprise, for

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.403
startups, for
everyone in between.

00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:15.070
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
as I understand it,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.570
one of the real
goals of Kubeflow

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:22.030
is to get these AI/ML tools in
the hands of data scientists

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.560
without those data
scientists also

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.570
having to be
Kubernetes-certified system

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.010
administrators, right?

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.260
MICHELLE CASBON: That's right.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.850
We want to provide access
for both DevOps and data

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.850
scientists, machine learning
engineers, everyone in between.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.610
So we don't want to take away
any control at the Kubernetes

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:37.180
layer.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.980
That's really important
for enterprises.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.397
But we don't want
a data scientist

00:01:40.397 --> 00:01:43.030
to have to learn about
Docker, and containers,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.590
and everything--

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.730
all the nuances of the
Kubernetes platform.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:51.880
So one of the first components
of Kubeflow was JupyterHub.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.070
And we've really
expanded on that

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.440
to make it possible
for data scientists

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.390
to not really ever
leave that environment,

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:01.900
to be able to build a model,
train it, and deploy it

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.670
directly from within
an environment

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.510
that they're familiar with.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.600
And so the idea
is that DevOps has

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.520
a lot of fine-grained
controls, but they

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.040
empower data scientists
to do everything

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.940
they need to get their work
done in a scalable way that

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.220
is sustainable, where
everyone's happy,

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.005
everyone's sort of
living in the space

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:21.880
that they're comfortable
in, and not stepping

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.178
on each other's toes.

00:02:23.178 --> 00:02:24.970
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
in terms of community,

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.000
and the community
contributions around Kubeflow,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.088
Google fits into
that, of course.

00:02:30.088 --> 00:02:31.630
Maybe tell us a
little bit about what

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:32.950
Google does in the
community, and where

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.320
else you see community
participation in the Kubeflow

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.205
project.

00:02:36.205 --> 00:02:38.320
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
so Kubeflow started out

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.850
as a Google project.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.250
A year and a half ago it
was launched at KubeCon.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.350
And we've seen an
incredible uptick

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.530
in the number of contributors,
number of PRs that we've seen.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.140
One of my favorite
new features was just

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.570
debuted a week ago at
our community call.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.120
We had a few
researchers in Italy,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.495
they showed this demo of some
notebook features, so something

00:02:58.495 --> 00:03:00.370
that makes it even easier
for data scientists

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.100
to essentially add
tags to a notebook

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.340
so that it will automatically
run within pipelines.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.440
And this whole story,
the more people

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.180
use the platform,
the more they come up

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.610
with ideas that we
haven't considered before,

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.280
and the more that
community base grows.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.125
So we had a really successful
happy hour last night.

00:03:18.125 --> 00:03:19.750
I think there were
only a few Googlers.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.570
But we packed this tiny
neighborhood bodega.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.940
There were people spilling
out into the streets.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.610
And it was people from
all over the world.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.850
And that's really
encouraging to see.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.270
We have a lot of big
support from Azure.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.308
We have a bunch of
AWS integration.

00:03:36.308 --> 00:03:38.350
It doesn't just run on
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.527
It's designed to be a
portable platform for running

00:03:41.527 --> 00:03:43.860
machine learning anywhere you
have a Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.300
It uses vanilla Kubernetes APIs.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.070
And the influx of people
coming from all over and adding

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.800
to that has been
incredibly encouraging.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.540
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, I can see it.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.868
And tell me a little bit
about the applications

00:03:56.868 --> 00:03:58.660
you're starting to see,
maybe some the ones

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.910
you're most excited about
seeing developed on top

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.645
of the Kubeflow platform.

00:04:02.645 --> 00:04:05.020
MICHELLE CASBON: So the thing
that I'm most excited about

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:05.800
is--

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.650
part of the keynote was
a few people from CERN

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.900
who came up and talked
about this public data set

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.130
that they have.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.040
So they replicated the
Higgs boson experiment,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.149
and they did that on GKE.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.390
And so I'm meeting
with them very soon.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.060
I get to actually visit
CERN and take a tour.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.910
I've been wanting to do
this for so many years.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.190
If you've seen the
documentary "Particle Fever,"

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.282
it is incredible.

00:04:28.282 --> 00:04:29.740
And so I get to
see that in person.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.950
And I'm hoping that, when
I meet with the team,

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.787
that I can come up
with some sort of way

00:04:35.787 --> 00:04:37.120
to get them running on Kubeflow.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.370
Because it would be amazing
to work with that data set

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.470
and to sort of help them
along their journey.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.740
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Michelle,
thank you very much.

